{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1539752-can-metis-cubs-still-potentially-have-the-ability-to-change-at-birth","id":1539752,"title":"Can Metis cubs still potentially have the ability to change at birth?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-08-03T09:12:50-07:00","id":1539752,"title":"Can Metis cubs still potentially have the ability to change at birth?","content":"I don\u2019t recall seeing mention of this in 20th, and I\u2019m not even sure if it came up in revised. Just wondering, because I always loved the idea of some metis having that ability, as well as the ability to shape-shift better than other Garou.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","name":"Mercurial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar986_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-03T12:21:48-07:00","id":1539762,"title":"","content":"Yes, they can. Guardians of the Caerns has a version of the merit that says this;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Guardians of the Caerns p.105</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Metis with this Merit are born able to shift their forms from one to the other. Metis such as this are the ones you hear about who turn from human infants to wolf cubs while still in the crib. (Fengy, from the Central Park setting, is one example.) Even when a metis with this Merit is knocked unconscious, the difficulty to assume whatever form she chooses is reduced to 7. Nobody knows their body and its limitations better than a Metamorph-blessed metis.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-03T13:18:15-07:00","id":1539763,"title":"","content":"If you are talking about Metis cubs shape changing at will prior to their First Change (as oxymoronic that would be), I don't think that's a thing.<br /> <br /> However, there are some canon examples of metis cubs changing into a non-metis form quickly after birth. Feng from the Sept of the Green who assumed the lupus form is one example as is Hacktalon from Valkenburg Foundation who assumed a homid form. In neither case are we given to believe they could keep changing forms though prior to their &quot;First Change&quot; when all the powers of the Garou come forth.<br /> <br /> We're not told that this is normal, but neither are we told this is abnormal. Most likely these 1e examples were done as part of a backstory so that writers didn't have to think through the problems a Metis birth provides for a setting that is all about keeping the Veil. So as an ST, I utilize the concept whenever I feel it is needed, but don't use if I want to deal with the issues of a metis birth. Just another storytelling tool.<br /> <br /> That addendum to the Metamorph Merit found in Guardians of the Caern that <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar110_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"110\" data-vbnamecard=\"110\">Ana Mizuki</a> mentions, is probably just a retroactive attempt to clarify why Fengy could do that. Fengy certainly did not have that Merit when created. So while I do think it's a valid excuse to why a metis might change soon after birth into another form, I would not limit myself to that. There could be many different reasons why a metis spontaneously changes to another form soon after birth. It could be the intervention by the spirits, something related to Ancestors, some kind of rite performed by someone, an instinctive supreme use of willpower to force a change upon recognition they are in danger, or even just be an unexplained mystery that Garou attribute to the love of Gaia.<br /> <br /> Personally, I think having any Garou be able to change prior to their First Change will lead to trouble in the setting. I would keep those metis post-birth spontaneous (one way) changes within reason. But obviously you can give any Metis that Metamorph merit. I know several players who really liked making their Metis PC be ultra-competent in shapechanging with that Merit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-03T15:15:04-07:00","id":1539767,"title":"","content":"In hindsight, I'm sort of surprised that there was never any sort of Rite for trying to bind a Metis child into either homid or lupis form.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}